WEBVTT

00:00.375 --> 00:03.795
She would always grab my hand after we finished the check-up.

00:04.671 --> 00:06.006
Her warm fingers,

00:06.214 --> 00:09.467
the exact shape of which I can only guess at to this day,

00:09.467 --> 00:14.389
pressed against mine with an urgent yet methodical tenderness,

00:14.931 --> 00:15.640
searching.

00:16.766 --> 00:20.645
I could see the skin at the corner of my palm sink gently

00:20.645 --> 00:23.565
where I should have caught sight of her thumb,

00:24.649 --> 00:29.404
the flesh beneath its translucent outline gradually yielding

00:29.404 --> 00:31.740
as it moved toward my index finger.

00:32.782 --> 00:38.371
After a while, her weary voice would softly describe the X-ray image she had obtained,

00:38.663 --> 00:44.627
her diagnosis each time revealing
something far beyond my bones.

00:45.295 --> 00:47.922
It was as if she could see right through me,

00:48.339 --> 00:52.677
which, in our case, filled me with additional unease.

00:53.261 --> 00:59.434
After all, it was I who was staring at a seemingly empty hospital bed,

00:59.434 --> 01:04.481
while I held out my palm towards her, as she was determined to read my future.

01:05.482 --> 01:11.362
I watched as the covers creased beneath the weight of the body occupying it.

01:12.113 --> 01:19.204
I could get lost in her vivid storytelling so easily and imagine her predictions so clearly that

01:19.204 --> 01:23.708
I often forgot I could not actually see her.

01:24.959 --> 01:30.673
I know it sounds strange, but despite tending to her body every day,

01:31.132 --> 01:33.510
she was, somehow, invisible.

01:34.594 --> 01:40.183
My eyes could not make out any visual image of her face or form,

01:40.975 --> 01:48.233
yet I can still recall the physiological existence that seemed to negate that absence.

01:49.692 --> 01:53.905
I remember how, every morning, when I entered the patient’s room, she was already awake.

01:54.823 --> 02:04.082
Soft, sigh-like murmurs hovered above the hollow her body had made in the stack of pillows,

02:04.332 --> 02:05.333
giving her away.

02:06.167 --> 02:08.419
I remember how, every morning,

02:08.419 --> 02:15.301
I was surprised by her voice coming from slightly lower than expected;

02:15.885 --> 02:22.600
I thought it was because she had shrunk a little with age, though I couldn’t say for sure.

02:22.892 --> 02:31.234
I remember how, every morning, I watched the disc-shaped resonator of my stethoscope leave my side,

02:31.234 --> 02:36.364
seemingly floating through the air as if on its own, until it came to rest in the right place against her skin,

02:36.406 --> 02:42.328
her steady pulse suddenly amplified in my ears.

02:42.912 --> 02:49.836
Sometimes the familiar lub-dub echoed so loudly that I couldn’t tell it apart from my own.

02:50.712 --> 03:00.221
I remember how, every morning, I felt her wrinkled fingers warm mine for a second as they took the thermometer from my hand,

03:00.221 --> 03:03.433
before it paused at some indeterminate point, suspended in the air.

03:03.808 --> 03:09.898
I watched the column of mercury rise
slowly as it responded to

03:09.898 --> 03:15.445
the warmth of the skin I couldn’t see, gradually
revealing whether she had a fever.

03:16.070 --> 03:23.661
And I remember how, one morning, a Saturday, I
believe, not long after all of our every-mornings,

03:24.078 --> 03:29.709
the hospital director’s statement resonated through the room I clearly saw her occupy.

03:30.126 --> 03:34.297
<i>»But, sir, this bill was clearly a mistake on our part!</i>

03:34.547 --> 03:36.716
<i>Your grandmother was never here.</i>

03:37.133 --> 03:38.509
<i>She was erased;</i>

03:39.010 --> 03:40.595
<i>she never existed«</i>

03:41.387 --> 03:43.514
It was as if she had completely vanished.
